<odoo>
    <!-- ``base.res_partner_rule`` (patched by ``partner_multi_company``'s
         post_init_hook to use ``company_ids``) always exempts any contact
         linked to an internal user (``partner_share = False``) from the
         company scoping, so a colleague's contact card, from another
         company or even the same one, stays visible to everyone. Global
         (no ``groups``) rules are ANDed together (see
         ``ir.rule._compute_domain``), so this rule tightens that exemption
         away without touching the existing one: it must still hold for the
         record to be visible, on top of the base rule.

         Deliberately no group-based exemption (e.g.
         ``base.group_multi_company``): the goal is that every user, no
         matter how many companies they are allowed into, only sees
         contacts belonging to a company they are actually assigned to:
         a user assigned to 2 out of 50 companies stays scoped to those 2.
         A real system administrator does not need a bypass here: install
         ``res_company_admin_sync`` and they are kept assigned to every
         company automatically, so the plain company-scoping condition
         below already lets them see everything.

         A user only gets to see an internal-user contact if: it is not
         actually internal (``partner_share = True``, e.g. a portal
         customer, untouched by this rule), it is their own contact (safety
         net), it has been deliberately shared (blank ``company_ids``, same
         "shared" convention as the rest of the stack), or it belongs to a
         company they are actually assigned to. Hiding administrators
         specifically is a separate, non-multi-company concern: see
         ``partner_hide_admin_contact``.

         Deliberately ``user.company_ids`` (every company the user
         belongs to) and NOT the bare ``company_ids`` eval-context
         variable (``self.env.companies``, only the ones *currently
         active* in the company switcher): visibility must not depend on
         which companies happen to be checked in that dropdown right now.
         Odoo core has several built-in mechanisms that read another
         internal user's contact regardless of the acting user's active
         company selection, e.g. Discuss auto-subscribing every
         internal user to a channel when a new user is created
         (`mail/models/discuss/discuss_channel.py`,
         `_subscribe_users_automatically_get_members`) enumerates every
         user in the relevant group, not just the ones sharing the active
         company. Scoping by the active selection made those crash with
         an AccessError the moment an administrator had narrowed their
         switcher to fewer companies than they actually belong to. -->
    <record id="res_partner_rule_restrict_cross_company" model="ir.rule">
        <field
            name="name"
        >res.partner: restrict internal-user contacts to own company</field>
        <field name="model_id" ref="base.model_res_partner" />
        <field name="domain_force">
            ['|', '|', '|', ('partner_share', '=', True), ('id', '=', user.partner_id.id), ('company_ids', '=', False), ('company_ids', 'in', user.company_ids.ids)]
        </field>
    </record>
</odoo>
